{"type":"Feature","properties":{"kind":"port_limits","cargo":[],"osm":["way/398255535","way/398255534","way/934286257","way/934286249","way/934286251","way/934286256","way/934286258","way/934286252"],"source":"Derived: the convex hull of this port's terminals and way 934286252 from OpenStreetMap, grown by 200 m, then cut to the water OpenStreetMap maps inside it (the sea within its coastline, rivers, docks and basins), with the terminals and those areas kept whole","licence":"ODbL-1.0","reviewed_at":"2026-11-23","clip":"water","disjoint":"DP World's container terminal is mapped as two areas, Terminal A conteneurs on the water and its extension, a yard about 100 m back from the shore and 30 m from the terminal. Nothing OpenStreetMap maps joins the extension to the water, so it stands as a part of its own.","note":"OpenStreetMap maps no port limits for Dakar. These are the hull of the terminals below and Mole 10, the fishing mole on the west side of the harbour, from Mole 1 in the south to the container terminal extension in the north, grown by 200 m, then cut to the water OpenStreetMap maps inside it, with the terminals and Mole 10 kept whole and a 30 m margin where they meet the water. What is left is the moles and the harbour basins between them; the Plateau, the Médina and Gorée are outside. A scan of OpenStreetMap finds no homes, schools, hospitals, places of worship or named places inside. One residential area overlaps it: a 218 km² multipolygon that covers most of the Cap-Vert peninsula, the port included.","inside":{"houses":0,"residential_m2":0,"residential_too_large":[{"osm":"relation/7284072","km2":219.5}],"amenities":0}},"geometry":{"type":"MultiPolygon","coordinates":[[[[-17.421668,14.6719],[-17.421706,14.671629],[-17.421434,14.671561],[-17.42177,14.670081],[-17.421474,14.670024],[-17.420762,14.670161],[-17.420159,14.670551],[-17.419756,14.671135],[-17.419101,14.675231],[-17.420105,14.681396],[-17.420519,14.678788],[-17.420596,14.678794],[-17.420451,14.67